+# LeRobot Rollout for VLA-0
+
+This fork adds a LeRobot policy adapter for the HTTP inference service in
+[`mattpidden/vla0`](https://github.com/mattpidden/vla0). It lets VLA-0 control an SO-101 through
+`lerobot-rollout`, including recorded episodic evaluations and a different language prompt for every
+episode from a CSV file.
+
+The two projects run in separate environments:
+
+- **Terminal 1 — VLA-0 server:** loads the checkpoint and runs inference on the GPU.
+- **Terminal 2 — this repository:** reads the cameras, sends observations to the server, controls the
+  SO-101, records episodes, and uploads the resulting dataset to Hugging Face.
+
+This repository does **not** contain the VLA-0 model code or a checkpoint. Keep a separate checkout of
+Matt's VLA-0 repository for Terminal 1.
+
+## Install the LeRobot client
+
+The commands below create an isolated Python 3.12 environment and install the SO-101 and RealSense
+dependencies. Replace the clone URL only if you are installing from another fork.
+
+```bash
+git clone https://github.com/justintiensmith/lerobot_rollout_vla0.git
+cd lerobot_rollout_vla0
+
+conda create -n lerobot_rollout_vla0 python=3.12 -y
+conda activate lerobot_rollout_vla0
+
+python -m pip install uv
+uv pip install --python "$CONDA_PREFIX/bin/python" -e ".[feetech,intelrealsense]"
+```
+
+Verify the hardware dependencies and the installation:
+
+```bash
+python -c "import scservo_sdk, pyrealsense2; print('Feetech and RealSense imports OK')"
+python -m pip check
+lerobot-rollout --help
+```
+
+Log in once if rollouts will be uploaded to Hugging Face:
+
+```bash
+hf auth login
+hf auth whoami
+```
+
+## Terminal 1: start VLA-0
+
+Install [`mattpidden/vla0`](https://github.com/mattpidden/vla0) in its own environment by following
+that repository's README. Do not install its dependencies into `lerobot_rollout_vla0`.
+
+Then start the VLA-0 service from the VLA-0 repository, using the checkpoint you want to evaluate:
+
+```bash
+conda activate vla0
+export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
+cd /path/to/vla0
+
+export ROBOVERSE_DEPLOY_CHECKPOINT=/path/to/model_last.pth
+python rv_train/deploy/service.py
+```
+
+The service listens on port `10000` by default. Leave it running and confirm that it is ready:
+
+```bash
+curl http://127.0.0.1:10000/health
+```
+
+## Terminal 2: test one unrecorded rollout
+
+Activate the client environment and enter this repository:
+
+```bash
+conda activate lerobot_rollout_vla0
+cd /path/to/lerobot_rollout_vla0
+```
+
+This is the configuration tested on Hippasus. Camera indices are machine-specific, so check them with
+`lerobot-find-cameras opencv` and change `0` or `12` if necessary. Camera names and their order must
+match VLA-0 training: `wrist` first and `middle` second.
+
+```bash
+lerobot-rollout \
+  --strategy.type=base \
+  --inference.type=sync \
+  --policy.type=vla0 \
+  --policy.server_url=http://127.0.0.1:10000 \
+  --policy.n_action_steps=4 \
+  --robot.type=so101_follower \
+  --robot.port=/dev/ttyACM0 \
+  --robot.id=blue_follower \
+  --robot.use_degrees=true \
+  --robot.cameras='{
+    wrist: {type: opencv, index_or_path: 0, width: 640, height: 480, fps: 30, fourcc: MJPG},
+    middle: {type: opencv, index_or_path: 12, width: 640, height: 480, fps: 30}
+  }' \
+  --task="Push the apple to the block." \
+  --fps=10 \
+  --duration=0 \
+  --device=cuda
+```
+
+No `--policy.path` is needed: the checkpoint is loaded by the server. `policy.n_action_steps` is the
+number of actions consumed from each returned chunk before requesting another chunk.
+
+## Record and upload episodes
+
+Use the episodic strategy to save LeRobot episodes and upload them when the session exits normally.
+The following example records ten 60-second episodes with a 60-second reset period:
+
+```bash
+lerobot-rollout \
+  --strategy.type=episodic \
+  --inference.type=sync \
+  --policy.type=vla0 \
+  --policy.server_url=http://127.0.0.1:10000 \
+  --policy.n_action_steps=4 \
+  --robot.type=so101_follower \
+  --robot.port=/dev/ttyACM0 \
+  --robot.id=blue_follower \
+  --robot.use_degrees=true \
+  --robot.cameras='{
+    wrist: {type: opencv, index_or_path: 0, width: 640, height: 480, fps: 30, fourcc: MJPG},
+    middle: {type: opencv, index_or_path: 12, width: 640, height: 480, fps: 30}
+  }' \
+  --task="Push the apple to the block." \
+  --fps=10 \
+  --dataset.repo_id=justintiensmith/rollout_vla0_test \
+  --dataset.root=/path/to/datasets/rollout_vla0_test \
+  --dataset.num_episodes=10 \
+  --dataset.episode_time_s=60 \
+  --dataset.reset_time_s=60 \
+  --dataset.video=true \
+  --dataset.push_to_hub=true \
+  --dataset.private=false \
+  --device=cuda
+```
+
+For a fresh recording, LeRobot adds a timestamp to the supplied dataset name so an existing Hub
+dataset is not overwritten. The startup log prints the final repository ID and local root. Use that
+exact stamped ID and root together with `--resume=true` to continue an interrupted run.
+
+Episodic keyboard controls are:
+
+- **Right arrow:** finish the current episode or reset period early.
+- **Left arrow:** discard the current episode and record it again.
+- **Escape:** finish the session, finalize the dataset, and upload it.
+
+## Evaluate prompts from a CSV
+
+The CSV must contain a `Prompt` column. The other mapped columns are used for operator-facing logging
+and may be named on the command line. Each saved dataset episode advances to the next selected CSV
+row; discarding an episode reuses its prompt, and resuming a dataset continues from the next unsaved
+row.
+
+This example matches `Eval_Ordinal_Reference_Ordering.csv`:
+
+```bash
+lerobot-rollout \
+  --strategy.type=episodic \
+  --strategy.prompt_file="$HOME/Downloads/Eval_Ordinal_Reference_Ordering.csv" \
+  --strategy.prompt_column=Prompt \
+  --strategy.episode_column=Episode \
+  --strategy.scene_column="Scene Setup (P1-P4)" \
+  --strategy.condition_column=Condition \
+  --strategy.start_row=0 \
+  --strategy.num_rows=130 \
+  --inference.type=sync \
+  --policy.type=vla0 \
+  --policy.server_url=http://127.0.0.1:10000 \
+  --policy.n_action_steps=4 \
+  --robot.type=so101_follower \
+  --robot.port=/dev/ttyACM0 \
+  --robot.id=blue_follower \
+  --robot.use_degrees=true \
+  --robot.cameras='{
+    wrist: {type: opencv, index_or_path: 0, width: 640, height: 480, fps: 30, fourcc: MJPG},
+    middle: {type: opencv, index_or_path: 12, width: 640, height: 480, fps: 30}
+  }' \
+  --teleop.type=so101_leader \
+  --teleop.port=/dev/ttyACM1 \
+  --teleop.id=blue_leader \
+  --fps=10 \
+  --dataset.repo_id=justintiensmith/rollout_vla0_ordinal_reference_ordering \
+  --dataset.root=/path/to/datasets/rollout_vla0_ordinal_reference_ordering \
+  --dataset.num_episodes=130 \
+  --dataset.episode_time_s=60 \
+  --dataset.reset_time_s=60 \
+  --dataset.video=true \
+  --dataset.push_to_hub=true \
+  --dataset.private=false \
+  --device=cuda
+```
+
+`dataset.num_episodes` is the target total when a prompt CSV is used. To resume, replace the dataset
+repository ID and root with the exact values printed when the dataset was created, then add
+`--resume=true`.
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- `feetech-servo-sdk is required`: rerun `uv pip install -e ".[feetech,intelrealsense]"` in the
+  client environment.
+- `/health` fails: the VLA-0 service is not running, is using another port, or crashed while loading
+  its checkpoint.
+- The arm hardly moves: first confirm the exact prompt punctuation and both trained camera views.
+  Also inspect the action chunks printed by the VLA-0 server; this adapter sends the returned absolute
+  six-joint targets without converting them into relative actions.
+- The wrong camera opens: use stable `/dev/v4l/by-id/...` paths or rerun `lerobot-find-cameras
+  opencv` after reconnecting cameras.
+
+For implementation details, see [the VLA-0 adapter documentation](docs/source/vla0.mdx).
+
